    "turn_text": "right now. I believe Congress needs to act. I think there should be a national standard. There have been multiple bills introduced in Congress. I introduced legislation that was the product of spending many months listening to stakeholders, listening to universities, listening to conferences, listening to the NCAA, listening to players, and trying to really solicit best practices that the legislation I've introduced. Number one protects NIL rights for college athletes. I think that is fair and I think it's right that if you worked incredibly hard, if you've developed remarkable skills, you ought to be able to enjoy the fruits of your labor. And so I think it is a much more fair system that college athletes who have extraordinary talent should be able to reap the rewards from that. But at the same time, there needs to be a set of rules and a set of standards across the board. And my legislation empowers the NCAA to set those rules. There are different bills that have been filed that would create a governmental agency or a quasi-governmental agency to set those rules. I think that's the mistake. I think the last thing anyone should want is elected politicians deciding what constitutes pass interference. That would be a nightmare. The NCAA has its faults to be sure. But I think it is far better to have a governance system where the member institutions have a say and it is drawn from the stakeholders. And so my hope is that the recent settlement, the House settlement for the NCAA will provide an impetus for Congress to step in",
